      "text": "To find the new class mean, we need to calculate the total score of all the students and divide it by the number of students.\n\nFirst, let's determine the total score of the 30 students who took the test initially:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total score of first 30 students} = 30 \\times 72\\%\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate the total score of the remaining six students, each with a mean score of 78\\%:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total score of last 6 students} = 6 \\times 78\\%\n\\]\n\nNow, we combine these two sums to get the overall total score of all 36 students:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total score} = (30 \\times 72\\%) + (6 \\times 78\\%)\n\\]\n\n multiplying by a decimal (e.g., $0.72 \\text{ } \\text{ or } 0.78$) is the same as multiplying by 72\\% or 78\\%, as percentages are equivalent to their decimal forms when expressed as fractions with an unwritten denominator of 100. So, the calculation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total score} = \\left(30 \\times 0.72\\right) + \\left(6 \\times 0.78\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total score} = 21.6 + 4.68 = 26.28\n\\]\n\nFinally, to find the new class mean, we divide the total score by the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n\\text{New class mean} = \\frac{26.28}{36} \\% = \\boxed{72.44\\%}\n\\]",
